{"questions":[{"question_id":"hwqnaLtz64pNGhiP","paperTitle":"JEE Main 2013 (Offline)","year":2013,"examDate":2013,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"periodic-table-and-periodicity","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","topic":"ionization-energy","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"the-first-ionization-potential-of-na-is-51-ev-the-value-of-2013-marks-4-hwqnaltz64pnghip.htm","content":"The first ionization potential of Na is 5.1 eV. The value of electron gain enthalpy of Na+\n will be: ","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"– 5.1 eV "},{"identifier":"B","content":"–10.2 eV"},{"identifier":"C","content":"+ 2.55 eV"},{"identifier":"D","content":"– 2.55 eV"}],"correct_options":["A"],"explanation":"Ionization potential of Na means energy required to convert of Na to Na<sup>+</sup> ion.\n<br><br>Na <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Na<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>-</sup>          IE = 5.1 ev\n<br><br>Electron gain enthalpy of Na<sup>+</sup> means energy required to convert Na<sup>+</sup> ion to Na.\n<br><br>Na <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Na<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>-</sup>          <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.65ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.434ex\" height=\"2.27ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 2401.8 1003.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(864,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(466,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> = - 5.1 ev\n<br><br>According to Lavoisier and Laplace law of thermochemistry, when we change the direction of reaction then the sign of energy also changes."}]}